; AVR interrupt service routine — ATmega328P (the CI-simulatable classic part).
;
; Stage 14 AVR-5 (design/stage14/avr-targets.md §5). Demonstrates the ISR idiom:
; an interrupt handler is just a `defn` whose name is the avr-libc vector symbol
; `__vector_<N>`, carrying the LLVM `"signal"` function attribute so the AVR
; backend emits the interrupt prologue/epilogue (SREG save/restore + `reti`
; instead of a plain `ret`). avr-libc's crt provides the vector table with weak
; `__vector_N` defaults; defining the strong symbol overrides slot N — so the
; `.vectors` table entry N ends up jumping to our handler.
;
; Vector 13 on the ATmega328P is TIMER1_OVF (Timer/Counter1 Overflow;
; iom328p.h: `#define TIMER1_OVF_vect _VECTOR(13)`, and sfr_defs.h:
; `#define _VECTOR(N) __vector_ ## N`). So the handler is named `__vector_13`.
;
; `defisr` is NOT a macro in this compiler: top-level user macros are not
; expanded (the toplevel dispatch matches literal head symbols before any macro
; expansion), so a `defmacro` cannot introduce a top-level definition. The ISR
; idiom is therefore the two adjacent forms below — the generic `fn-attr`
; directive followed by the `defn` — with `fn-attr` FIRST (it is consumed at
; emit-defn time; there is no forward-reference prescan for the attribute table).
; See the block comment in lib/avr.nuc for the convention.
;
; `(exclude-prelude)` is no longer required (Stage 16 took the node/arena runtime
; out of the prelude); it is kept because this example also pins that path. Every
; call here is direct (no function *values*, an addrspace(1) verifier error on AVR).
;
; Build + link for the ATmega328P (a real link via the avr-gcc driver, AVR-3):
;   nucleusc --target=avr --mcpu=atmega328p examples/avr-isr.nuc -o isr.elf

(exclude-prelude)

(import-use "lib/avr.nuc")
(import-use "lib/avr/atmega328p.nuc")

; --- Timer/Counter1 Overflow ISR (vector 13) ---
; `fn-attr` must precede the `defn` it targets. The "signal" attribute is what
; makes LLVM's AVR backend end this function in `reti` (and save/restore the
; registers an interrupt handler must preserve).
(fn-attr __vector_13 "signal")
(defn __vector_13 ():void
  ; Toggle PB0 on each overflow (drives an LED / a scope pin). A plain MMIO
  ; read-modify-write through the volatile-pointer helpers — no libc, no heap.
  (reg8-toggle-bit! PORTB 0))

(defn main ():i32
  ; PB0 as output so the ISR's toggle is observable.
  (reg8-set-bit! DDRB 0)
  ; Enable Timer/Counter1 overflow interrupt (TIMSK1 bit TOIE1) and start the
  ; timer with the /1024 prescaler (TCCR1B bits CS12|CS10). A real program would
  ; also execute `sei` (global interrupt enable) — the compiler has no inline-asm
  ; spelling for it yet (out of scope for AVR-5), so on hardware the crt or a C
  ; shim would set it; the vector wiring and `reti` epilogue this example proves
  ; are independent of when interrupts are globally enabled.
  (reg8-set-bit! TIMSK1 TOIE1)
  (reg8-set-bit! TCCR1B CS10)
  (reg8-set-bit! TCCR1B CS12)
  (while true
    0)
  (return 0))
